Haakon Nilsen <haakonn@operamail.com> wrote in message news:<bprfp7$r7b$1@toralf.uib.no>...
> Cockatrices are probably the single most annoying things I've encountered in
> nethack so far (except perhaps for rust monsters).  In my newbier days I
> even ate one, not thinking.  Play fast, die young, leave a beautiful
> statue.  Even now I don't understand them completely:
> 
> * Carrying with you a lizard corpse is a kind of protection from them.  I've
> never heard any hissing, but I understand you'll turn to stone after
> they've bitten or touched you two or three times.  Does carrying a lizard
> corpse provide protection against that?

Eating the corpse of a lizard or any non-acidic monster (blobs,
jellies, and the like) will save you from a *trice's hissing attack
(when you start "slowing down"). You still lose intrinsic speed,
though, and *trice corpses will still stone you instantly on contact.
Lizard corpses are best for this because they don't rot or become
tainted.

> * Is it safe to attack them with a wielded weapon?  Am I right to think that
> only bare-handed attacks will turn you to stone?  (Except of course when
> they bite you)

You are correct. To my knowledge, the only way to be stoned by a
*trice without touching it is being hissed at. Just getting bitten
will _not_ stone you (presumably stoning is caused by touching the
flesh, not the beak). _Biting_ the *trice will cause instantaneous
stoning, of course (be careful when polymorphed into D or V).

> * When they've bitten or touched a couple of times, you're close to being
> turned to stone.  Am I right to think that eating a lizard corpse will
> reset this "counter"?  Also, say I run away and meet the cockatrice later
> on, will this counter have been reset because it's been a while?

See above. A cockatrice's bite attack carries no risk of stoning. You
might be thinking of the lizard corpse's anti-stun effect, which
reduces your stunned timeout to two turns.

> So far I've been killing them with daggers and arrows, but it takes a while,
> and I have to lure them into following me in circles so I can pick up the
> same daggers to reuse them :)  I'm quite grateful that they seem to stay in
> the flock and don't split up to chase me into corners.

The conventional wisdom is to confront cockatrices from a distance,
and that's what I did the first time I met them, but I haven't found
them to be all that dangerous alive. IIRC, the odds of a *trice's
hissing starting the stoning process are 3/10 (except on new moons, a
folklore reference, when it always happens). It then has to pass
whatever magic cancellation you may have, up to 98% fail for MC 3
(provided by mithril or most special cloaks). Even if you do feel
yourself slowing down, you have several turns to stop it by eating one
of the aforementioned foods or praying. It's fairly trivial to reduce
your chance of hiss-attack stoning to less than 1%, and even if it
does happen, it's one of the easiest insta-deaths to protect against
(lizards will show up WELL before you begin to see *trices, and IIRC
they always leave corpses).

No, 99% of cockatrice YASDs come after the beastie is dead. *trice
corpses are _dangerous_, so treat them with extreme caution. Leave 'em
alone unless there are glove-wearing/stoning resistant monsters which
might potentially wield them against you on the level.

